course: everyday
lesson: 505
title: Half- and step-siblings
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Families can join together. A half-brother or half-sister shares ONE parent with you: the same mum, or the same dad. A stepbrother or stepsister shares NO parent with you: they are the child of your parent's partner. A family made by joining two families is often called a blended family. In Brighton, Theo-Jack draws his family tree at school: two stepsisters, one half-brother, a mum, a stepdad and a dad, all lines and arrows. «That's a big family!» says his teacher. Every line is a person who belongs.
explain: Step-siblings start out as strangers who suddenly share a home. In Manchester, Lucas, 9, moves in with Mum, his stepdad and stepbrother Jaylon, 11. «This was my room first,» says Jaylon, mardy as anything («mardy» is Northern for sulky). They split the room down the middle with tape. Both are grumpy. It takes time; finding one thing you both like helps. In Toronto, Sofia meets her dad's partner's two girls at a barbecue. They're polite but awkward, until one says, «So, uh, you like Minecraft, eh?» Sofia relaxes a bit. In Wellington, Mila's stepbrother teaches her to skateboard: «Sweet as, ya got it!» («sweet as» = great). Step-siblings can become really close.
explain: Some step-siblings are hard work. In Dublin, Amelia's stepbrother tells her, «You're not my real sister, ye sap.» It hurts. He may be upset about the new family too, but everyone in a family deserves respect: Amelia can tell a parent. In Atlanta, Malachi's stepbrother Trey is the cool kid, and at school Trey ignores him. «It's giving stranger,» Malachi mutters (Gen Z slang for «he's acting like a stranger»). School and home can be different worlds; talking at home helps. In Swansea, when Evie visits Dad, his partner's daughter Delphine has her own room and Evie sleeps on the sofa bed. She feels like a guest in her own dad's house. She can tell Dad: a shelf or a drawer just for her might help.
explain: Half-siblings can be close or barely know each other. In Leeds, Jake-Lee's mum has a baby with her new partner. People say «half-sister». Jake-Lee says, «She's my sister, not half,» and feels protective. You can call a sibling whatever feels right to you. In Hull, Ruby-Jean only sees her half-brother at Christmas; he's 14 and calls her «kid». She'd like to know him better, so she can ask to message or see him more. In Chicago, Darnell has a half-brother in Texas he's never met; they FaceTime on Sundays. «Y'all got a pool?» Some half-siblings only know each other online at first, and might meet one day.
fact: A half-brother or half-sister shares one parent with you.
fact: A stepbrother or stepsister shares no parent with you; they are the child of your parent's partner.
fact: A family made by joining two families is often called a blended family.
fact: Step-siblings start as strangers forced together; it takes time, and a shared interest helps.
fact: Step-siblings can become close friends.
fact: «You're not my real sister» hurts; everyone in a family deserves respect, and a child can tell a parent.
fact: Step-siblings at the same school may act differently there than at home; talking at home helps.
fact: A child who visits a parent's home can feel like a guest; having a space of their own, even a drawer, can help.
fact: You can call a half-sibling or step-sibling whatever feels right to you, like just «my sister».
fact: Some half-siblings live far apart and know each other only by video calls at first.
fact: «Mardy» is Northern English for sulky, «sweet as» is New Zealand for great, «ye sap» is Irish for you fool, and «it's giving» is Gen Z slang for «it looks like» or «it seems like».
exercise: What is a half-sister? | A sister who shares one parent with you | A sister who shares no parent with you | A sister who is exactly half your age, in years | A sister who lives with you half the week
exercise: What is a stepbrother? | The son of your parent's partner | A brother who shares both parents | A brother who is one year older, one step up | A cousin who lives in your house
exercise: What is a blended family? | Two families joined together | A family that makes smoothies | A family with only twins | A family that moves house a lot
exercise: Jaylon says, «This was my room first.» What do the boys do? | Split the room with tape | Swap houses for a week | Move into the garden shed | Sleep in the same bed
exercise: «Mardy as anything.» What does «mardy» mean? | Sulky | Happy | Muddy | Hungry
exercise: What helps step-siblings like Lucas and Jaylon get on? | Time, and one thing they both like | Keeping the tape on the floor for ever | Never speaking to each other | Their parents splitting up
exercise: What broke the ice for Sofia at the barbecue? | A question about Minecraft | A big fight over the burgers | Her dad telling a joke | Nobody spoke all day
exercise: Mila's stepbrother says, «Sweet as, ya got it!» What is he doing? | Teaching her to skateboard | Telling her off for falling over | Asking her for sweets | Taking her skateboard away
exercise: Amelia's stepbrother says, «You're not my real sister, ye sap.» What can Amelia do? | Tell a parent | Call him worse | Pack her bags | Agree with him
exercise: Why might Amelia's stepbrother be so mean? | He may be upset about the new family too | He was born mean and can't ever change | Amelia did something to him on purpose | Stepbrothers have to be mean, it's the rule everywhere
exercise: Trey ignores Malachi at school. What does the lesson suggest? | Talk at home; school and home can be different worlds | Follow Trey around school until he notices him at last | Tell the whole school Trey is his stepbrother right now | Ignore Trey at home as well, to pay him back for it
exercise: Evie sleeps on the sofa bed at Dad's and feels like a guest. What could help? | A shelf or drawer just for her | Never visiting Dad's again | Moving into Delphine's room for good | Sleeping in the car
exercise: Jake-Lee says, «She's my sister, not half.» Is that okay? | Yes, he can call her what feels right | No, he must always say «half-sister» | No, she's not related to him at all | Only when she's old enough to talk
exercise: How do Darnell and his half-brother keep in touch? | FaceTime | Letters by pigeon | They don't | Only at Christmas
exercise: Ruby-Jean wants to know her half-brother better. What can she do? | Ask to message or see him more | Wait until he's a grown-up and then ask him | Pretend she doesn't care | Only speak at Christmas
test: «stepsister = half-sister, same thing innit». What's the difference? | A half-sister shares one parent; a stepsister shares none | A stepsister shares both your parents; a half-sister one | They're exactly the same thing with two different names | A half-sister lives with you, a stepsister never does
test: «Lucas and Jaylon used tape, they'll hate each other forever lol». What's more likely? | It takes time, and they may get on later | They'll stay enemies for the rest of their lives | Tape always fixes everything between brothers | One of them will have to move to another house
test: What would probably happen if Amelia told her mum about the «not my real sister» comments? | The grown-ups could help, as everyone deserves respect | She'd get the blame for telling on her stepbrother | Nothing; parents can't help with step-siblings | Her stepbrother would be sent away to live elsewhere
test: «Trey ignores Malachi at school so he must hate him». What's a fairer view? | School and home can be different worlds | Trey definitely hates him and always will | Malachi should start ignoring everyone | Trey doesn't know Malachi goes there
test: In a fairy tale the stepsisters are always cruel. What does Mila's skateboarding show? | Step-siblings can become close | Fairy tales are always true to life | Only real sisters are kind | Stepsisters must be mean
test: «Darnell's never met his half-bro so he's not really his brother». What's true? | Half-siblings can start online and might meet one day | You only count as brothers if you've met in person | Half-brothers in different states aren't related | FaceTime friends can't be family under any rule
test: ★★★ Theo-Jack's mum married Rob, who has 2 daughters. Mum and Rob then had a baby boy. Who is the baby to Rob's daughters? | Their half-brother | Their stepbrother | Their cousin | Not related to them at all
test: ★★★ Lucas's mum married Jaylon's dad. Which statement is true? | Lucas and Jaylon share no parent | Lucas and Jaylon share one parent | Lucas and Jaylon share two parents | Jaylon is Lucas's half-brother
test: ★★★ Ruby-Jean is 9 and her half-brother is 14. In how many years will he be twice her age? | Never; it already happened 4 years ago | In 5 years | In 4 years | In 9 years
test: ★★★ Theo-Jack has 2 stepsisters, 1 half-brother and no full siblings. How many of these share at least one parent with him? | 1 | 3 | 2 | 0
test: «Evie's a guest at her dad's, that's just how it is». What does the lesson say? | She can tell Dad; a space of her own can help | She should accept it and stop visiting Dad | Only the kid who lives there can have space | She should demand Delphine's room for herself
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship of half-siblings? | They share precisely one parent | They share both parents | They share no parent | They share only a surname
exam: Identify the correct designation of the child of one's parent's spouse, where no parent is held in common. | A step-sibling | A half-sibling | A full sibling | A cousin
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the formation of relations between step-siblings. | Such relations require time and are assisted by a common interest | Such relations are fixed upon the first meeting and cannot alter | Such relations are invariably hostile, as related in fairy tales | Such relations may be formed only where the children share a room
exam: It is given that a child, upon visiting a parent's household, feels himself a mere guest. Ascertain the remedy proposed in the lesson. | To inform the parent, a space of one's own being of assistance | To discontinue all visits to the said household | To occupy the room of the resident child forthwith | To conceal the feeling from all members of the household
